WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.672
Coding Exercise 3 combines row filtering and group filtering.

00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:09.297
Use sales. First, keep only source rows where price is at least twenty-five.

00:00:09.297 --> 00:00:11.972
Then group the remaining rows by category.

00:00:11.972 --> 00:00:15.622
Calculate total quantity and total revenue for each category.

00:00:15.622 --> 00:00:20.867
After those groups exist, keep only categories whose total quantity is greater than ten.

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:24.122
Finally, sort with the highest revenue first.

00:00:24.122 --> 00:00:27.612
Think about the order conceptually before you write the clauses.

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:30.512
The price condition belongs to individual rows.

00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:33.412
The total-quantity condition belongs to groups.

00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:37.237
Run and Check when you are ready.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:41.622
If the result does not match, verify the placement of WHERE and HAVING before changing the aggregate

00:00:41.622 --> 00:00:48.172
expressions. For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:48.172 --> 00:00:50.387
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:50.387 --> 00:00:55.542
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:55.542 --> 00:00:58.282
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:05.357
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:05.357 --> 00:01:11.732
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:11.732 --> 00:01:14.017
not only whether the statement executes.
